t acquired TechGlobal", "MARIA CHEN:CEO of TechGlobal", "NEW YORK:City where the partnership was announced", "TECHGLOBAL:", "HOSTAGE EXCHANGE:Hostage exchange involving Aurelians in Firuzabad, negotiated by Quintara, finalized with funds transferred to Krohaara) <|COMPLETE|>", "PEIRCE:PEIRCE, referring to Charles Sanders Peirce, is an American philosopher and logician who founded semiotics and developed a comprehensive triadic theory of signs. He posited a triad of sign forms—firstness, secondness, and thirdness—and proposed a triadic classification of images within signs. Central to his framework is the idea that a sign is an image that stands for another image (its object) through an interpretant, which itself is another sign, thereby generating an infinite chain of sign mediation. This cognitive view ties signs to knowledge: signs function to make relations more efficient and intelligible.\n\nPeirce distinguished semiotics from purely linguistic determinants, arguing that perception, movement, and sign-interpretation operate together within a dynamic system of images and signs. In his account, signs are not mere verbal tokens but complex mediators that link experiences to objects through interpretants, enabling a chain of understanding that extends beyond language alone.\n\nThe descriptions also reference specific sign types attributed to Peirce, such as dicisign, reume, and engramme, highlighting his nuanced taxonomy of signs beyond simple linguistic categories. Moreover, his theory is connected to cinema and visual rhetoric through the notion of movement-image categories—perception-image, action-image, relation-image—illustrating how sign theory can be applied to analysis of moving images and visual media.\n\nOverall, PEIRCE is presented as the principal founder of semiotics, whose triadic sign theory and image-based conception of signs place cognition and interpretation at the heart of meaning-making, underscoring the continuous interaction between perception, signs, and objects within an interconnected system of images and signs.", "MARCEL CARNE:French film director whose work, including Visiteurs du Soir and Les Enfants du Paradis, is discussed in the text as emblematic of poetic realism, notable for its use of light, mime, and atmospheric nuance within the French cinematic style.", "JOSEPH L. MANKIEWICZ:American screenwriter and director praised in the text for non-linear flashback storytelling, described as the greatest flashback author; associated with films such as The Barefoot Contessa and All About Eve.", "JORGE LUIS BORGES:Argentine writer whose short story The Garden of Forking Paths is cited in the text as describing time as a web of forks and multiple possibilities.)<|COMPLETE|>", "CHRONOS:Chronos is the Greek mythological personification of time, mentioned in contrast to Cronos within Bergson's discussion.)<|COMPLETE|>", "GARREL:GARREL is a film director referenced in the discussion of experimental cinema, noted for using over- and under-exposed imagery and for exploring the bodily postures and presence in Garrel's works", "NOEL BURCH:NOEL BURCH is a film critic/scholar cited for his analysis of how black and white screens function in cinema, shaping the relationship between image and absence in experimental film"], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_74d1fdadc2aefa1000c956284b235c95606f82d09919b8598ff9e6f073c55692_v2"}